2013-02-19 02:58 – Jonadab the Unsightly One:
> On Feb 18, 9:42 am, Klaus Kassner <Klaus.Kass...@ovgu.de> wrote:
>> Who would be interested in such a quest artifact?
>> What kind of *real benefits* would it carry?
> While we're at it, can we ask those questions about
> the Heart of Ahriman?  Seriously.
> 
>> Since being burdened or stressed on the deeper
>> dungeon levels is dangerous, I would probably still
>> throw it away immediately.
> Personally, I generally leave the Bowling Ball of Fate in
> the quest, for exactly this reason.  Other people spend
> artifact wishes on it, though, so I guess not everyone
> approaches inventory management the same way.
> 
> I liked the idea of the convict patch's artifact iron
> ball that provided phasing.  The player really has to
> think about whether such a heavy thing is worth carrying,
> but phasing is very hard to obtain otherwise (except via
> polyself, which has its own drawbacks) and rather
> interesting (potentially useful, even), so it's worth
> thinking about.  Having to make trade-off decisions like
> that adds depth to the game.  The Orb of Fate, similarly,
> offers half damage from both spells and physical attacks,
> so some players choose to carry it.  The fact that some of
> us choose *not* to carry it tells you that it's a balanced
> item.  It's clearly useful, but it's not so potent as to be
> an overwhelming advantage.
> 
> So I think the idea of an artifact chest is not an
> inherently bad idea.  It just needs to have some compelling
> benefit to make it worth at least thinking about hauling
> the thing around.
> 
> If all it did was give you gold and gems, it would clearly
> not be worth carrying around.  If it gave you wishes every
> thousand turns, it would be overpowered.  The trick is to
> strike the right balance, somewhere in between.  I'm not
> sure I know exactly what the right balance would be (I
> don't have a lot of experience carrying chests around long
> distances, so I'm not sure exactly how badly that's going
> to cramp your inventory), but I can give you the general
> principle:  if the benefit it provides is balanced, there
> will be disagreement between players about whether the
> thing is worth carrying around or not.  Aim for that.
Hmm, maybe a magical chest that has a good deal less weight than the
“normal” chest, but creates an (additional) object inside it every
rnl(X) turns when carried.  Maybe biased towards the more useful object
types.  That would provide a benefit, but at the cost of never being
able to estimate exactly when the weight increase will make you
burdened.  It might occur right next to that minotaur, when you were
counting on a free turn…

